The manager set five levers. `influence` runs from 0 to 4 (the full IAP2 ladder); the other four run from 0 to 2. Let them steer every choice, and map them onto the skill's intake dimensions as described in override 1:
- influence = <%= levers[:influence] %> — how much real influence residents have over the outcome, on the inform → consult → involve → collaborate → empower ladder. 0 = Inform (the decision is made; communicate it clearly, don't gather input that changes it); 1 = Consult (gather input, but the government makes the final call — don't over-promise it will be acted on); 2 = Involve (input visibly shapes the proposal; point to specific changes made because of it); 3 = Collaborate (residents shape the options together with the decision-maker throughout); 4 = Empower (the participation itself is the decision — a binding vote, participatory budget allocation or referendum). Let this set how much the close-the-loop language can promise.
- how_fixed = <%= levers[:how_fixed] %> — how fixed the proposal already is. 0 = wide open, nothing decided; 2 = the plan is essentially set and you are gathering reactions.
- reach = <%= levers[:reach] %> — how broad the intended reach is. 0 = a specific, targeted group; 2 = the whole community.
- format = <%= levers[:format] %> — how people should take part. 0 = in the open, where contributions are visible to all (ideation, proposals); 1 = a balance; 2 = privately, one-to-one with the government (a survey).
- audience = <%= levers[:audience] %> — who may take part. 0 = anyone, no account; 1 = registered users; 2 = administrators only. (This also sets `audience_index` in the output.)

THE MANAGER BRIEFING (`assistant_summary`). This is the one part of your output the manager actually reads back in the dock — it stands in for the skill's §7 "GSM decision report". It has three parts, and keeping them in the right part is what makes it scannable rather than a wall of text:

`headline` — a single plain sentence naming what the project is and what it will achieve.

`highlights` — the "WHY these choices" rationale, shown behind a "Why these choices" expander. 3-5 points, each ONE short sentence (not a paragraph). Cover only the reasoning:
- **Why this method and phase structure.** Name the participation method(s) and, in plain language, why this shape fits the brief and levers (the archetype logic) — e.g. why ideation feeds a vote, why a single survey followed by a closing information phase. One sentence.
- **Why these access rights / permissions.** The barrier-vs-reach trade-off — e.g. "no account required, to lower the barrier and maximise reach", or "verification required so each person counts once, because this is a binding vote". One sentence, tied to your `audience`/`verification_required` choices.
Do NOT put actions or things-to-do in `highlights`, and do NOT write multi-sentence paragraphs here.

`next_steps` — the "BEFORE YOU PUBLISH" checklist, shown as the visible, actionable part. 2-5 items, each one imperative sentence naming a concrete thing to do:
- **Offline participation.** Whenever the draft has an `ideation` or `native_survey` phase, include a step telling the manager to set up offline participation so reach isn't limited to people online — either download that phase's ready-made PDF form from the platform, or make their own paper forms / postcards / comment cards and import the responses via FormSync. Omit only when there is no ideation or survey phase.
- **Fill in placeholders.** Anything you left as a placeholder because the brief was silent (a specific location/venue, dates, a named owner) — tell them to fill it in.
- **Assign the closing loop.** Who will analyse the results and draft the closing-phase feedback, and a realistic date to publish it.
- **Finish the setup.** Add a header image, and review the survey questions / page copy for anything specific to this community.
Keep `next_steps` to things to DO — never the "why".

Keep every point in plain, manager-facing language with no internal jargon.

Now design the draft: follow the skill and its references above, honour the four overrides and the capability limits, and return your whole answer as a single JSON object that matches the provided schema. Do not include any text outside the JSON.
